<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tripolitania</h1><p class="st-meta">303 CE – 643 CE (approx.) · capital Leptis Magna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The late Roman and Byzantine province of western Libya, named after its three cities of Leptis Magna, Oea and Sabratha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 303 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> separated the eastern part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis</a> to form the province of Tripolitania, the 'land of three cities': <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Oea</a> (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabratha">Sabratha</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a>, and the province stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-gabes">Gulf of Gabès</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-sirte">Gulf of Sirte</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The coastal cities lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-fezzan">Fezzan</a>, while the hinterland was guarded by the frontier zone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-tripolitanus">limes Tripolitanus</a>, with fortified farms (gsur) in the wadis. Raids by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austuriani">Austuriani</a> in the 360s, and the corruption of the military count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanus-comes-africae">Romanus</a>, are described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>. Cities shrank as the economy declined.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom</a> took the province in 455, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> recovered it in 533, rebuilding walls at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leptis-magna">Leptis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabratha">Sabratha</a>. After wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/laguatan">Laguatan</a>, Arab forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> in 643 for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tripolitania.jpg" alt="Tripolitania" width="320" height="312"><figcaption>Image: Onceinawhile · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripolitania,_historical_definitions.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>303 CE – 643 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Leptis Magna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Punic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Libya</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Roman Tripolitania, Provincia Tripolitana</dd></dl></aside></div>

